# Code style & patterns (core packages)

This rule applies to `packages/*` — the framework-agnostic core, wrappers,
and CLI. For Nuxt code in `apps/dashboard/`, `apps/www/`, or `packages/nuxt/`,
see nuxt-dashboard.mdc.

## Language & runtime

- TypeScript strict mode. No `any` without explicit justification in a comment.
- Node.js 22 LTS as target. Use modern syntax (ES2024+).
- ESM modules only. No CommonJS in new code.

## Types over comments

````ts
// ❌ Avoid
// userId is a UUID v7 string
function getUser(userId: string) { ... }

// ✅ Prefer
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: 'UserId' };
function getUser(userId: UserId) { ... }
````

Branded types for IDs, especially: `AgentId`, `SessionId`, `EventId`, `UserId`.

## Import the schema, don't redefine it

Types that exist in `schemas/*.json` are sourced from there. Use JSON Schema
to TypeScript generation (e.g., `json-schema-to-typescript`) so the runtime
contract and the compile-time type are the same thing.

````ts
// ❌ Avoid hand-writing this
interface AuditEntry {
  id: string;
  timestamp: string;
  // ... drift waiting to happen
}

// ✅ Prefer generated types
import type { AuditEntry } from '@/generated/audit-entry';
````

## Async patterns

- Always async by default. The firewall hot path is async.
- Use `Promise.all` for parallelizable layer checks where order doesn't matter.
- Never block the hot path on external services. If something might be slow,
  it goes off the critical path (background queue, fire-and-forget).

## Error handling

- Throw typed errors. Define them in `packages/core/src/errors.ts`.
- The firewall never crashes the agent. If the firewall itself fails, it
  defaults to BLOCK (fail-safe) and logs the internal error to the audit log.

````ts
class FirewallInternalError extends Error {
  constructor(public layer: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5, message: string) {
    super(`[Layer ${layer}] ${message}`);
  }
}
````

## Naming

- Layer functions: `classifyIntent`, `checkRateLimits`, `detectAnomaly`,
  `requestApproval`, `recordAudit`. Verb-first, action-oriented.
- Types: PascalCase for types, camelCase for values.
- Constants: `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` only for true compile-time constants.
- Tools and tool categories: lowercase with dots, e.g., `gmail.send`.

## Tests

- Behavioral tests live in `test/behavioral/`. They describe *what* the
  firewall should do, not *how* it does it.
- Unit tests live in `test/unit/`. They test internals.
- Test names start with "should" or "must" and describe observable behavior.
- Reproduce real incidents as tests: name them after the incident.

````ts
describe('Layer 3 — mass action detection', () => {
  it('should block the OpenClaw scenario (50+ deletes in 60s)', async () => {
    // ...
  });
});
````

## Performance discipline

- Any code in the hot path (intent classification, rate limit check) must
  have a benchmark in `bench/`.
- p95 target: under 200ms for the full pipeline. Per-layer targets:
  - Layer 1: ~30ms
  - Layer 2: ~5ms
  - Layer 3: ~80ms
  - Layer 5: ~10ms
  - Layer 4: variable (human-in-the-loop)

If you add latency to any layer, you must:
1. Justify it in the PR description.
2. Add or update the benchmark.
3. Update the doc if the target shifts.

## Dependencies

- Each new dependency requires explicit justification.
- Prefer zero-dep solutions when reasonable (we use `@noble/ed25519` for
  exactly this reason — no native deps).
- Never depend on a framework in the core package. Framework integrations
  are separate packages.
- **Critical**: core packages must NEVER import from `nuxt`, `vue`,
  `@vue/*`, `nitropack` or any frontend framework. If you need to share
  types with the frontend, expose them from `@agent-firewall/core` and let
  the frontend consume them.

## Comments

- Comments explain *why*, not *what*. The code shows the what.
- Reference docs when relevant: `// See: docs/concepts/anomaly-detection.md#repeated-message`
- Reference ADRs when the choice is non-obvious: `// See: ADR-0007`
